## Why This Is Asked
This tests how a junior engineer models read-only environments, data sourcing, and per-env state management without touching prod resources. It mirrors real-world patterns at scale at top tech companies.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform data sources for read-only references
- Per-env backends to segregate state
- Avoiding resource creation in isolated environments

## Code Example
```javascript
data "aws_vpc" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "tag:Environment"
    values = ["inventory"]
  }
}
data "aws_subnet" "inventory" {
  filter {
    name   = "vpc-id"
    values = [data.aws_vpc.inventory.id]
  }
}
```

```javascript
terraform {
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "tf-state"
    key    = "inventory/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}
```

```javascript
output "inventory_vpc_id" { value = data.aws_vpc.inventory.id }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you verify that inventory plans never propose changes to prod data? 
- How would you automate inclusion of inventory in CI without accidentally applying prod changes?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests knowledge of multi-account Terraform setups with minimal code duplication, demonstrating proficiency with provider aliases, wrapper modules, and CI gates to enforce environment boundaries.

## Key Concepts
- Provider aliases for multi-account AWS access
- Backend/workspace separation for state isolation
- Wrapper modules for environment context management
- CI gates to enforce production change protections

## Code Example
```hcl
terraform {
  required_version = ">= 1.0"
  backend "s3" {
    bucket = "terraform-state-bucket"
    key    = "${terraform.workspace}/terraform.tfstate"
    region = "us-east-1"
  }
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "prod"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

provider "aws" {
  alias  = "sandbox"
  region = "us-east-1"
}

module "environment" {
  source = "./modules/environment-wrapper"
  providers = {
    aws = terraform.workspace == "prod" ? aws.prod : aws.sandbox
  }
  environment = terraform.workspace
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical understanding of per-env isolation using workspaces, ensuring policy via gates, not just code.

## Key Concepts
- Terraform workspaces
- GCS backend prefixing
- Wrapper modules for consistent labeling
- GitHub Actions plan gating with json + jq

## Code Example
```javascript
// wrappers/env_wrap/main.tf (skeleton)
variable 'environment' {}
variable 'cost_center' {}
```
```javascript
// backend.tf (skeleton)
terraform {
  backend 'gcs' {
    bucket = tf_state_bucket
    prefix = terraform/${terraform_workspace}
  }
}
```
```javascript
// gate.sh (skeleton)
terraform init
terraform plan -out=tfplan
PLAN_JSON=$(terraform show -json tfplan)
# simple policy: if changes exist beyond tag updates, fail
```

